# Benchmark corpora are measurement data, not source text.
#
# Without this file Git normalises every "text" file to LF on commit and
# converts it back to CRLF on a Windows checkout. For source that is harmless.
# For a compression corpus it is not: the bytes ARE the experiment, and a file
# that gains one byte per line on one platform is a different input there.
#
# This was not hypothetical. benchmarks/canterbury/alice29.txt is natively
# CRLF; normalisation stored it as LF, so the repository held 148,481 bytes
# where the Canterbury corpus defines 152,089. The other four files in that
# directory are natively LF and were stored correctly, which is why the
# discrepancy went unnoticed -- it affected exactly one file in five.
#
# Marking the corpus directories -text stops all conversion in both
# directions: the bytes on disk are the bytes in the object database, on every
# platform. Do not remove these lines without re-verifying every corpus file
# against benchmarks/corpus_manifest.json.

benchmarks/canterbury/**            -text
benchmarks/canterbury_official/**   -text
benchmarks/corpus/**                -text
benchmarks/documents/**             -text
benchmarks/govdocs1/**              -text
benchmarks/large/**                 -text
benchmarks/silesia/**               -text